    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d581">

                <pb facs="0905" n="883" sameAs="frus:frus1951v02/pg883"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.42/4–1751</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Minister of Embassy in Canada</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Bliss</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Ambassador in Canada
                    </hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Woodward</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ottawa</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-04-17">April 17, 1951</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Subject: Pearson’s Speeches.</p>
                <p>Haselton has informed me by telephone that Tony Satterthwaite<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d581-f1"/> proposes to telephone you tomorrow regarding the United
                    States reaction to Pearson’s speeches on the subject of relationship between
                    Canada and the United States. It appears that Pearson’s statements have received
                    considerable attention in the Department of State at the Assistant Secretary
                    level, even at the time when the MacArthur affair<anchor n="2" xml:id="d581-f2"
                    /> might have been expected to monopolize attention.</p>
                <pb facs="0906" n="884" xml:id="pg884"/>
                <p>Tony appears to think it desirable to let Pearson know that his views have been
                    carefully noted in the State Department, have been widely discussed, and have
                    received careful attention. He does not propose that you argue the matter with
                    Pearson or give him the impression that we are unduly concerned. However, it
                    might contribute to a more friendly attitude if you could convey to Pearson, in
                    a joshing manner, the impression that his viewpoint has received full attention
                    in the State Department.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d581-f1" n="1" xml:id="d581-f1-content">
                <p>Livingston Satterthwaite, Deputy Director, Office of British Commonwealth and
                    Northern European Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d581-f2" n="2" xml:id="d581-f2-content">
                <p>On April 11, General of the Army Douglas MacArthur was relieved of his command of
                    United States Army forces in the Far East and United Nations forces in Korea.
                    For documentation on this subject, see volume <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >vii</hi>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
